      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\nLooking at the code, I need to analyze the global variables `i` and `h` for atomicity violations following the <Write, Read, Write> pattern.\n\nLet me trace through the execution:\n\n**Main function (Priority 0):**\n- Line 43: `i = 11;` (Write to i)\n- Line 45: `h = plus(x, -17);` (Write to h)\n- Line 47: `h = x;` (Write to h)\n- Line 49: `i = 3;` (Write to i)\n\n**ISR (svp_simple_702_001_isr_1) - Higher Priority:**\n- Line 26: `y = h;` (Read from h)\n- Line 32: `z = i;` (Read from i)\n\nNow I need to check for <Write, Read, Write> patterns:\n\n**For variable `i`:**\n- a1: Line 43 - Write `i = 11` (main function)\n- a2: Line 32 - Read `z = i` (ISR - higher priority)\n- a3: Line 49 - Write `i = 3` (main function)\n\nThis forms a valid <Write, Read, Write> pattern where the ISR can preempt between the two consecutive writes in main and read the intermediate value.\n\n**For variable `h`:**\n- a1: Line 45 - Write `h = plus(x, -17)` (main function)\n- a2: Line 26 - Read `y = h` (ISR - higher priority)\n- a3: Line 47 - Write `h = x` (main function)\n\nThis also forms a valid <Write, Read, Write> pattern where the ISR can preempt between the two consecutive writes in main and read the intermediate value.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [43, 32, 49]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"h\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [45, 26, 47]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLooking at the code, I need to analyze the accessibility of each read/write operation mentioned in the defect report.\n\n**Step 1: Check Control Conditions**\n\nFor variable `i` (lines 43, 32, 49):\n- Line 43: `i = 11;` is inside `if (x > -1000